<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Primož Trubar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1508–1586</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Primož Trubar was a Slovene Protestant reformer and the founder of the Slovene literary language. In 1550, in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, he published the first two printed books in Slovene, a catechism and a primer, and he later led the Protestant church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>. He translated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> and wrote over twenty books, and he is regarded as a central figure of Slovene culture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/primoz-trubar.jpg" alt="Primož Trubar" width="320" height="485" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jakob Lederlein · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Primoz-Trubar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
